---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="About & attribution"
  description="Where the Tioman climbing guidebook comes from, who to credit, and how to have material corrected or removed."
  section="/"
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/#areas">Guidebook</a><span>/</span><span>About</span>
    </nav>

    <div class="detail-hero" style="border-bottom:0">
      <p class="label">About</p>
      <h1>Where this comes from</h1>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Pulau Tioman has a genuinely world-class rock climbing area — the Dragon's Horns, the twin
        granite towers above Kampung Mukut — and yet no single guidebook has ever existed for it.
        Route information has been scattered across a paper logbook at Mukut, expedition reports,
        magazine articles, blog posts and a few films. This site exists to gather it into one
        place and keep it current.
      </p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>The guidebook is a <strong>community compilation</strong>. It draws on:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>the <strong>climbers' logbook</strong> kept at Kampung Mukut, written by many parties;</li>
        <li>
          the <strong>"Tioman Climbing Route Book"</strong>
          (<a href="https://github.com/therizhao/tioman-climbing/blob/main/dragonhorns-climb-log.pdf">PDF</a>)
          compiled by <strong>Tam Haja</strong>, which itself gathered typed topos, photographs of
          logbook pages, and published notes;
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>expedition and first-ascent reports</strong> — the Cedar Wright / Lucho Rivera notes,
          David Kaszlikowski / Vertical Vision material, the Iranian "Mountains for Peace" team,
          Malaysian and Chinese teams, and visiting climbers;
        </li>
        <li>trip reports and films hosted on their authors' own sites, linked from the <a href="/logbook">logbook</a>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We try to credit every author, first-ascent team and photographer. Where a source is
        uncertain or internally inconsistent, we say so on the page rather than paper over it.
      </p>

      <h2>Generated with the help of AI</h2>
      <p>
        Parts of this guidebook were transcribed and drafted by AI tools working from scans of
        handwritten pages. That process can introduce <strong>plausible-looking errors</strong> — a
        grade that was never given, a misread name, a pitch inferred from an ambiguous topo. If you
        spot one, please <a href="/contribute">correct it</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Takedown</h2>
      <p>
        If you hold copyright in any text, topo or photograph used here and you want it credited
        differently or removed, email <a href="mailto:rizhaow@gmail.com">rizhaow@gmail.com</a> and we
        will respond and act promptly.
      </p>

      <h2>Licence</h2>
      <p>
        Original contributions to this project — our transcriptions, structured data and any topo
        line-art we draw ourselves — are intended to be shared under a permissive climbing-community
        licence (proposed: CC BY-SA 4.0). This does not override the rights of third parties in
        material they created.
      </p>

      <h2>Contributing</h2>
      <p>
        Everything lives in one public repository:
        <a href="https://github.com/therizhao/tioman-climbing">github.com/therizhao/tioman-climbing</a>.
        Open a pull request, or email the address above.
      </p>

      <h2>Emergency</h2>
      <p>
        Going climbing here? Read the <a href="/emergency">emergency &amp; rescue page</a> and save
        the local contact — <strong>Tam Haja, +60 12-919 0785</strong>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="height:clamp(2rem,5vw,4rem)"></div>
</Base>
